Create a Fresh Ubuntu VM

Open VMware Workstation

Click Create a New Virtual Machine

Choose Typical

Select:

Installer disc image file (ISO)

Browse and select your Ubuntu ISO (e.g., ubuntu-22.04.3-live-server.iso)

OS Type:

Linux → Ubuntu 64-bit

Set:

VM Name: ubuntu-template

Location: your VM storage path

Allocate:

CPU: 2 vCPU

RAM: 2–4 GB

Disk: 30–40 GB (Single file is fine)

Finish and power on.

Install Ubuntu normally:

Use a generic username like template

Do not install personal tools

Enable OpenSSH only if you need it in all clones

2️⃣ Clean the VM for Template Use

Login to the VM and run:

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt autoremove -y
sudo apt clean


Remove machine-specific data:

sudo rm -f /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*
sudo truncate -s 0 /etc/machine-id
sudo rm -f /var/lib/dbus/machine-id
sudo ln -s /etc/machine-id /var/lib/dbus/machine-id


Clear logs and temp files:

sudo rm -rf /var/log/*
sudo rm -rf /tmp/*
sudo rm -rf /var/tmp/*


Remove shell history:

history -c
rm -f ~/.bash_history


Shutdown the VM:

sudo poweroff

3️⃣ Convert It Into a “Template”

In VMware Workstation:

Right-click the VM → Manage → Clone

Choose:

Clone from the current state

Create a full clone

Name it something like: ubuntu-template-base

Keep this VM powered off forever

This is now your gold image.

4️⃣ Creating New VMs from the Template

Whenever you need a new Ubuntu machine:

Right-click ubuntu-template-base

Clone

Choose:

Full Clone

Name it (e.g., k8s-node-01, nextcloud-host, etc.)

Start it

On first boot, Ubuntu will:

Regenerate machine-id

Regenerate SSH host keys

Act like a brand-new system

Fix it with:

sudo ssh-keygen -A


or, if the package state is broken:

sudo dpkg-reconfigure openssh-server


Then start SSH:

sudo systemctl restart ssh
sudo systemctl status ssh
